    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the absolute first-line treatment for moderate-to-severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea in adult patients?', '["Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP)", "Continuous Positive Airway Pressure (CPAP)", "Oral appliances (mandibular advancement devices)", "Hypoglossal nerve stimulation", "Nasal septoplasty"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CPAP is the gold standard, first-line therapy for moderate-to-severe adult OSA. It provides a pneumatic splint that keeps the airway continuously patent, virtually eliminating all apneas and hypopneas if worn consistently.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does a CPAP machine physically prevent upper airway collapse during sleep?', '["It acts as a mechanical vacuum, sucking air out of the lungs", "It delivers a continuous stream of pressurized room air, creating a pneumatic splint that keeps the pharyngeal walls from collapsing", "It selectively stimulates the genioglossus muscle", "It acts as a physical bite block", "It cools the airway to make it rigid"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CPAP does not breathe for the patient. It simply supplies a constant column of positive air pressure that holds the collapsible soft tissues of the pharynx apart, preventing collapse.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the standard clinical definition of "good compliance" (adherence) for CPAP therapy used by Medicare and insurance companies?', '["Using the machine at least 1 hour per night", "Using the machine at least 4 hours per night for at least 70 percent of nights in a consecutive 30-day period", "Using the machine only on weekends", "Using the machine at least 8 hours every single night", "The patient reporting they feel better"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Insurance coverage and compliance standards define adequate adherence as ≥4 hours of use per night on ≥70% of nights. Unfortunately, real-world long-term compliance rates are historically low (often around 50-60%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is the most common reason patients report for discontinuing or failing to tolerate CPAP therapy?', '["The machine is too heavy to lift", "Mask discomfort, claustrophobia, skin irritation, and difficulty exhaling against high pressures", "It causes vivid nightmares", "It is too expensive to run", "It makes snoring louder"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Mask fit issues, air leaks, skin breakdown, and claustrophobia are the primary barriers to compliance. Working closely with sleep clinics to trial different masks (nasal pillows, full face masks) is key to solving this.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient using CPAP complains of severe dry mouth, throat irritation, and nasal congestion in the mornings. What is the most effective initial adjustment to improve compliance?', '["Increase the CPAP pressure", "Switch to an oral appliance", "Add heated humidification and ensure proper mask seal to prevent air leaks", "Stop therapy immediately", "Prescribe oral antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dryness is usually caused by unhumidified air or an air leak (especially mouth breathing with a nasal-only mask). Adding a heated humidifier and a chin strap (or switching to a full face mask) typically resolves this.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Under what clinical scenario is Bilevel Positive Airway Pressure (BiPAP) typically preferred over standard CPAP?', '["For patients with mild OSA who snore loudly", "For patients who require very high pressures (e.g., >15 cm H2O) and struggle to exhale against the high continuous pressure of CPAP, or those with concurrent hypoventilation/COPD", "BiPAP is always first-line for everyone", "For pediatric OSA only", "For patients with TMJ disorders"]'::jsonb, 1, 'BiPAP provides two different pressures: a higher Inspiratory Positive Airway Pressure (IPAP) to open the airway, and a lower Expiratory Positive Airway Pressure (EPAP) to allow easy exhalation. This pressure relief increases tolerance at high settings.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does an Auto-adjusting Positive Airway Pressure (APAP) machine differ from a standard CPAP machine?', '["APAP is battery-operated only", "APAP continuously adjusts the delivered pressure throughout the night using algorithms that detect snoring, flow limitation, and apneas, delivering the minimum pressure needed", "APAP requires a tracheostomy", "APAP does not use a mask", "APAP only works during REM sleep"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard CPAP delivers a single fixed pressure all night. APAP increases the pressure when it senses obstruction (e.g., when the patient rolls onto their back or enters REM sleep) and lowers it when the airway is stable, often improving patient comfort.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 42-year-old female with mild OSA (AHI = 8) refuses CPAP due to severe claustrophobia. She has healthy teeth and no joint problems. What is the most appropriate alternative non-surgical treatment?', '["Tracheostomy", "A Mandibular Advancement Device (MAD) / Oral appliance", "Systemic steroids", "Daily nasal sprays", "Weight gain"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For patients with mild-to-moderate OSA who cannot tolerate CPAP, or those who prefer them, custom-made oral appliances are an excellent, highly effective option.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does a Mandibular Advancement Device (MAD) work to prevent obstruction during sleep?', '["It physically holds the lips closed", "It pulls the lower jaw (mandible) and the attached tongue base forward, physically expanding the retrolingual (hypopharyngeal) airway", "It paralyzes the soft palate", "It pushes the epiglottis down", "It delivers electrical stimulation to the tongue"]'::jsonb, 1, 'By securing to the upper and lower teeth and holding the mandible in a protruded position, it keeps the tongue base from falling back against the throat wall.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is a common, long-term side effect of using a Mandibular Advancement Device (MAD)?', '["Vocal cord paralysis", "Dental shifts, malocclusion (changes in bite), and temporomandibular joint (TMJ) pain", "Loss of taste", "Chronic sinusitis", "Middle ear effusion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because MADs place significant mechanical force on the teeth and jaw joint, patients frequently experience transient morning jaw stiffness, and over years, can develop permanent changes in their dental alignment or TMJ pain. Regular dental follow-up is mandatory.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A custom-made, titratable oral appliance is significantly more effective than "over-the-counter" boil-and-bite devices. What does "titratable" mean in this context?', '["The device dissolves in water", "The degree of mandibular protrusion can be incrementally adjusted forward over weeks to maximize airway opening while minimizing jaw pain", "The device measures oxygen levels", "The device adjusts its own pressure", "The device can be heated up"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Titration allows the dentist to slowly bring the mandible forward (e.g., in 1mm steps) until snoring/apneas stop, ensuring the patient''s TMJ has time to adapt to the new position.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'For which patient would an oral appliance (MAD) be strictly contraindicated?', '["An obese patient", "A patient with severe periodontal disease, loose teeth, or fewer than 10 healthy teeth per arch", "A patient with mild OSA", "A patient who snore without apneas", "A patient with a high Epworth score"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An oral appliance relies entirely on the teeth for anchorage. If the teeth are loose, diseased, or missing, the mechanical forces of jaw protrusion will cause severe dental damage or device failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary mechanism of action of "Positional Therapy" for patients with positional OSA?', '["It teaches the patient to sleep standing up", "It uses physical barriers (e.g., a tennis ball sewn into the back of a shirt, or specialized smart vibrating collars) to prevent the patient from rolling onto their back (supine)", "It elevates the feet above the heart", "It requires sleeping without a pillow", "It paralyzes the neck muscles"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For patients whose apneas occur strictly or primarily when sleeping on their back, keeping them in a lateral (side) position is highly effective, cheap, and avoids CPAP mask issues.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the relationship between weight loss and AHI in obese patients with OSA?', '["Weight loss has no effect on AHI", "A 10 percent reduction in body weight is associated with an approximate 26 percent reduction in the AHI", "Weight loss actually increases the AHI", "Weight loss only works if paired with nasal surgery", "Weight loss converts OSA to central sleep apnea"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Numerous studies have shown a strong, dose-dependent relationship. A moderate reduction in weight significantly decreases neck fat, expands the airway lumen, and can often downgrade severe OSA to mild or cured status.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is heavy alcohol consumption before bed highly detrimental to patients with OSA?', '["It causes insomnia", "It acts as a pharyngeal muscle relaxant, increasing airway collapsibility, and it elevates the arousal threshold, making apneic events longer and hypoxic drops deeper", "It makes the CPAP machine blow too hard", "It causes direct nasal congestion only", "It triggers restless legs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Alcohol relaxes the airway muscles, making collapse easier, and sedates the brain, preventing it from waking up quickly to breathe. This combination leads to dangerously long apneas and severe oxygen desaturation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How do sedating medications (like benzodiazepines, muscle relaxants, and sleeping pills) affect a patient with untreated severe OSA?', '["They cure sleep apnea by relaxing the patient", "They are highly dangerous because they depress central respiratory drive and delay the protective arousal response, potentially leading to fatal prolonged airway obstruction", "They improve CPAP compliance without side effects", "They shrink the tonsils", "They have no effect on sleep"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Patients with untreated OSA rely on micro-arousals (waking up for a split second) to save their lives when they choke. Sedatives prevent these arousals, leading to severe carbon dioxide retention, asphyxia, or death during sleep.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following cardiovascular conditions has the strongest, most direct, and independent causative link to untreated Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Mitral valve prolapse", "Systemic Hypertension (specifically resistant, non-dipping hypertension)", "Aortic stenosis", "Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy", "Pericarditis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Up to 80-90% of patients with drug-resistant hypertension (failing three or more medications) have underlying, undiagnosed OSA. Treating the OSA with CPAP frequently results in a significant, lasting drop in blood pressure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "non-dipping" blood pressure, a classic finding in patients with severe untreated OSA?', '["Blood pressure that drops too low during exercise", "A pathological failure of the blood pressure to drop by 10-20 percent during sleep, caused by constant sympathetic surges from nighttime choking", "Blood pressure that changes when standing up", "Normal blood pressure", "Low blood pressure at noon"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Normally, blood pressure "dips" during sleep as the body rests. In OSA, constant hypoxia and arousals keep the sympathetic nervous system firing all night, keeping blood pressure high and drastically increasing the risk of stroke and heart attack.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following cardiac arrhythmias is most commonly triggered, exacerbated, or made resistant to medical therapy by untreated severe OSA?', '["Ventricular fibrillation", "Atrial Fibrillation (A-Fib)", "Mobitz Type I block", "Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome", "Junctional rhythm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The recurrent sympathetic surges, hypoxia, and atrial stretch from negative thoracic pressure create a perfect substrate for A-fib. Patients undergoing A-fib ablation have a much higher recurrence rate if their underlying OSA is left untreated.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does untreated severe OSA contribute to the development or worsening of Congestive Heart Failure (CHF)?', '["By causing structural damage to the heart valves", "By increasing left ventricular afterload (due to systemic hypertension and massive negative intrathoracic pressure), leading to myocardial hypertrophy, remodeling, and systolic/diastolic dysfunction", "By directly poisoning the heart muscle", "By decreasing fluid volume in the body", "By causing pericardial effusion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The heart has to work much harder to pump blood against high systemic pressure and against the vacuum-like negative pressure in the chest during obstructed inhalation. This chronic strain leads to heart failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Untreated severe OSA is an independent risk factor for which devastating cerebrovascular event?', '["Cerebral aneurysm rupture", "Ischemic Stroke", "Multiple sclerosis flare", "Parkinson''s disease", "Brain abscess"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The combination of hypertension, vascular inflammation, endothelial dysfunction, and nocturnal hypoxia dramatically increases the risk of stroke. CPAP therapy significantly mitigates this risk.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does chronic intermittent hypoxia in OSA affect the endothelial lining of blood vessels?', '["It makes blood vessels wider", "It triggers oxidative stress and systemic inflammation, impairing nitric oxide release and leading to endothelial dysfunction, vasoconstriction, and accelerated atherosclerosis", "It increases blood vessel elasticity", "It has no effect on blood vessels", "It causes blood vessels to grow new cartilage"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hypoxia turns on inflammatory pathways, releasing cytokines that damage the vascular endothelium. This is the root cause of the widespread cardiovascular disease seen in OSA patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which pulmonary vascular pathology can develop as a direct consequence of severe, chronic untreated sleep apnea?', '["Pulmonary embolism", "Pulmonary Hypertension and right-sided heart strain (cor pulmonale)", "Pulmonary fibrosis", "Asthma", "Pneumothorax"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every time the lungs go without oxygen, the pulmonary arteries constrict (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction). Doing this hundreds of times a night eventually leads to permanent remodeling, high pulmonary pressures, and right heart strain.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Untreated OSA is strongly linked to which major endocrine/metabolic disorder, independent of the patient''s weight?', '["Type 1 Diabetes", "Insulin Resistance and Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus", "Addison''s disease", "Hyperparathyroidism", "Diabetes insipidus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The chronic elevation of cortisol and catecholamines (stress hormones) from nighttime choking events impairs insulin sensitivity and glucose uptake, making blood sugar very difficult to control.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the relationship between OSA and Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)?', '["OSA completely prevents reflux", "The massive negative intrathoracic pressure generated as the patient struggles to inhale against a closed airway acts like a siphon, physically sucking gastric acid up into the esophagus and larynx", "CPAP causes acid reflux", "Reflux only occurs in patients with central sleep apnea", "There is no link"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Many patients with severe "heartburn" or hoarseness/throat clearing (LPR) at night find that their reflux completely vanishes once they start using CPAP, as the negative thoracic vacuum is eliminated.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which hepatic condition is highly prevalent and independently associated with the severity of Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Hepatitis B", "Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) / Non-Alcoholic Steatohepatitis (NASH)", "Wilson''s disease", "Liver cirrhosis from alcohol only", "Hemochromatosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Intermittent hypoxia directly promotes fat accumulation, inflammation, and fibrotic changes in the liver, accelerating the progression of NAFLD to NASH independently of obesity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old truck driver presents with severe daytime sleepiness. What is the most critical public safety concern regarding this patient?', '["He might lose his job", "An extremely high risk of motor vehicle accidents (drowsy driving) due to impaired vigilance, slow reaction times, and microsleeps behind the wheel", "He will sleepwalk", "He will get a headache", "He will develop a rash"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Drowsy driving is a major killer. OSA patients are 2 to 7 times more likely to be involved in a car crash than healthy individuals. In many jurisdictions, physicians are legally required to report drivers with untreated severe OSA to licensing bodies.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What are "microsleeps" in a patient with severe sleep deprivation from OSA?', '["Sleeping for 5 minutes", "Brief, uncontrollable episodes of sleep lasting from a fraction of a second up to 30 seconds, during which the patient is completely unaware of their surroundings", "A type of dream", "A symptom of CPAP use", "A type of seizure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Microsleeps occur without warning when the brain is severely sleep-deprived. If this happens while driving, the results are catastrophic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which neurocognitive deficit is most commonly observed in patients with chronic, untreated Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Loss of long-term memory", "Impaired executive function, reduced short-term working memory, difficulty concentrating, and slowed mental processing speed", "Aphasia", "Apraxia", "Complete dementia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The combination of chronic sleep fragmentation and repeated hypoxia physically damages the prefrontal cortex, leading to severe cognitive "brain fog" that typically resolves with CPAP therapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does untreated OSA affect a patient''s mental health and mood?', '["It has no effect on mood", "It is strongly associated with an increased risk of developing clinical Depression, irritability, and severe anxiety", "It causes bipolar mania", "It cures depression", "It causes schizophrenia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because sleep is vital for emotional regulation, chronic sleep disruption in OSA often presents as treatment-resistant depression or generalized anxiety. Screen for OSA in any patient with resistant depression.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA complains of severe, throbbing, bilateral headaches upon waking up in the morning. How long do these classic morning headaches typically last after the patient gets out of bed and begins breathing normally?', '["They last all day", "They typically resolve spontaneously within 30 minutes to 1 hour as the patient hyperventilates and blows off the retained CO2", "They require high-dose opioids to resolve", "They only occur during the winter", "They last for exactly 12 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Morning headaches in OSA are driven by hypercapnia-induced cerebral vasodilation. Once awake and breathing normally, the CO2 levels return to normal, vasoconstriction occurs, and the headache fades.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the impact of CPAP therapy on sexual function and erectile dysfunction (ED) in men with OSA?', '["CPAP worsens erectile dysfunction", "CPAP therapy significantly improves erectile dysfunction and can increase libido, likely by reducing sympathetic overactivity, improving endothelial function, and normalizing testosterone levels", "CPAP has no effect on sexual function", "CPAP causes permanent impotence", "CPAP only works if the mask is worn during sex"]'::jsonb, 1, 'OSA is a major, treatable cause of ED. The improved oxygenation and reduced vascular stress from CPAP can restore normal erectile function.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which major metabolic cluster of conditions is highly prevalent in patients with OSA, combining abdominal obesity, hypertension, dyslipidemia, and insulin resistance?', '["Cushing''s syndrome", "Metabolic Syndrome (Syndrome X)", "Polycystic ovary syndrome only", "Graves'' disease", "Conn''s syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'There is a massive pathophysiological overlap between OSA and Metabolic Syndrome, with each condition worsening the other in a vicious cycle.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does the chronic negative intrathoracic pressure of OSA affect the physical structure of the aorta?', '["It shrinks the aorta", "It places repetitive mechanical stress and stretching on the ascending aorta, which is a known risk factor for the development of thoracic aortic aneurysms and aortic dissection", "It makes the aorta stiffer and immune to aneurysms", "It has no effect on the aorta", "It causes aortic valve stenosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every time the patient chokes, the negative pressure in the chest stretches the aorta like a balloon. Over years, this mechanical stress can lead to aneurysmal dilation or life-threatening tearing (dissection).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe heart failure is found to have "Cheyne-Stokes breathing" on their sleep study. What does this indicate?', '["Severe obstructive sleep apnea", "A classic pattern of Central Sleep Apnea characterized by a crescendo-decrescendo pattern of tidal volume separated by central apneas, driven by unstable feedback loops in the respiratory center", "A normal breathing pattern", "Vocal cord paralysis", "Asthma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cheyne-Stokes respiration is a form of central sleep apnea common in severe congestive heart failure. It is driven by a delayed circulation time between the lungs and the brainstem chemoreceptors, causing massive swings in respiratory drive.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What specialized positive airway pressure machine is specifically designed and highly effective for treating Cheyne-Stokes breathing in heart failure patients?', '["Standard fixed CPAP", "Adaptive Servo-Ventilation (ASV)", "Bilevel PAP with no backup rate", "Nasal high-flow oxygen", "A simple snore guard"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASV machines continuously monitor the patient''s breathing volume. If the patient hypoventilates or stops breathing (during the decrescendo/apnea phase), the machine steps in and breathes for them. When they hyperventilate (crescendo), the machine backs off, stabilizing the breathing pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is Adaptive Servo-Ventilation (ASV) strictly contraindicated in patients with severe, symptomatic heart failure who have a reduced ejection fraction (LVEF ≤ 45 percent)?', '["The machine is too heavy", "The landmark SERVE-HF trial showed that ASV therapy was associated with an increased risk of cardiovascular mortality in this specific patient population", "It causes severe ear infections", "It prevents the patient from coughing", "It makes the heart pump too fast"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a critical safety rule in sleep medicine. For patients with systolic heart failure (LVEF ≤ 45%), ASV is contraindicated. Standard CPAP or BiPAP must be used instead.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA is undergoing surgery for a broken leg. Why is the anesthesiologist highly concerned about the post-operative recovery period?', '["The patient will wake up too fast", "Anesthesia and post-op narcotics suppress airway reflexes and muscle tone. Without CPAP, the patient is at an extremely high risk of airway obstruction, asphyxiation, and death in the recovery room", "The patient will snore and wake other patients", "The patient will refuse to eat", "The patient''s surgical wound will not heal"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Post-op OSA monitoring is a critical safety issue. The combination of residual anesthesia, pain meds (opioids), and lack of CPAP can be fatal. OSA patients must have their CPAP used in the PACU.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "CPAP-induced aerophagia"?', '["A type of lung infection", "The uncomfortable swallowing of air into the stomach, leading to severe abdominal bloating, gas, and pain, often caused by excessively high CPAP pressures", "A feeling of suffocation", "A dry nose", "An allergic reaction to the mask plastic"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Aerophagia is a common compliance barrier. If the CPAP pressure exceeds the opening pressure of the upper esophageal sphincter, air is pushed into the stomach. Lowering the pressure, switching to APAP, or using medication can help.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is considered a safe, non-pharmacological, behavioral recommendation for all patients diagnosed with sleep apnea?', '["Sleeping on their back", "Avoiding alcohol and sedatives, particularly within 4-6 hours of bedtime", "Eating a heavy meal right before bed", "Taking a warm bath immediately before sleep", "Using earplugs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Avoiding respiratory depressants before bed is a universal, highly effective safety measure for all OSA patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does smoking tobacco affect the severity of Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Smoking cures OSA by stiffening the tissues", "Smoking causes direct airway inflammation, mucosal edema, and increased nasal resistance, which significantly worsens upper airway collapsibility", "Smoking has absolutely no effect on sleep", "Smoking causes central sleep apnea only", "Smoking acts as a respiratory stimulant during sleep"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Smoking irritates the delicate tissues of the throat, causing swelling (edema) that narrows the airway lumen and makes it floppier.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Can CPAP therapy cure the underlying anatomical causes of Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Yes, it permanently shrinks the tongue", "No, CPAP is a supportive, stenting therapy; if the patient stops using the machine, the airway will immediately resume collapsing during sleep", "Yes, but only if used for 10 years", "Yes, it removes fat from the neck", "Yes, in children only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CPAP is like glasses: it only works while you wear it. It does not structurally remodel the jaw or remove tissue.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the role of oral "mandibular advancement devices" in the management of severe OSA?', '["They are the primary first-line therapy for all severe cases", "They are generally reserved as a second-line therapy for severe patients who have completely failed or refused CPAP, as they are less reliable than CPAP in severe disease", "They do not work in severe disease at all", "They are only used to stop snoring, not apneas", "They are placed in the nose"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While MADs are highly effective for mild-to-moderate OSA, they often struggle to maintain airway patency in severe cases (AHI > 30). They can be trialed in severe patients who absolutely refuse or cannot tolerate CPAP.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which specialist should custom-fabricate and manage a Mandibular Advancement Device to ensure proper fit and monitor for dental complications?', '["A general practitioner", "A qualified sleep dentist working in conjunction with the patient''s sleep physician", "An ENT surgeon", "A speech therapist", "A physical therapist"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Oral appliance therapy requires specialized dental knowledge to protect the TMJ and dental alignment. A qualified sleep dentist must design and fit the device.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient using CPAP at a pressure of 12 cm H2O develops a middle ear effusion (fluid in the ear). What is the physiological mechanism?', '["The CPAP mask is leaking into the ear canal", "Pressurized air from the nasopharynx is forced up the Eustachian tube, causing barotrauma, mucosal edema, and fluid accumulation in the middle ear space", "The CPAP is causing a viral infection", "The patient has severe allergies", "The CPAP is melting the ear bones"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Eustachian tube connects the throat to the middle ear. High positive pressure in the throat can back up into the ear, causing pressure pain or fluid accumulation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA has been using CPAP successfully for 6 months. What changes would you expect to see in their daytime blood pressure?', '["Their blood pressure will increase", "A clinically significant reduction in both systolic and diastolic blood pressure, particularly at night, reducing their overall cardiovascular risk", "No change in blood pressure", "A dangerous drop in blood pressure causing fainting", "Their blood pressure will fluctuate wildy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'CPAP therapy is one of the most effective non-pharmacological treatments for reducing blood pressure in hypertensive OSA patients, often allowing them to reduce their medication dosages.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is standard oxygen therapy (delivering oxygen via nasal cannula without pressure) NOT an acceptable primary treatment for Obstructive Sleep Apnea?', '["Oxygen is toxic during sleep", "While oxygen may improve oxygen saturations slightly, it does not prevent the physical collapse of the airway, the massive respiratory effort, the sympathetic surges, or the severe sleep fragmentation", "Oxygen causes central sleep apnea", "Oxygen makes snoring louder", "Oxygen is too expensive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Oxygen therapy is a band-aid. It stops the blue color, but the patient is still choking, struggling to breathe, and constantly waking up all night. The structural blockage must be resolved.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does CPAP therapy affect a patient''s overall quality of life, mood, and cognitive performance?', '["It makes them more depressed", "It leads to dramatic improvements in daytime alertness, cognitive function, memory, mood, and overall vitality", "It has no measurable effect on quality of life", "It causes permanent memory loss", "It makes them hyperactive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'By restoring normal sleep architecture and eliminating nighttime hypoxia, CPAP can completely transform a patient''s life, resolving chronic fatigue and cognitive fog.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a high AHI is diagnosed with OSA but states, "I don''t feel tired at all during the day." Should they still be treated?', '["No, treatment is only for tired patients", "Yes, because asymptomatic patients with moderate-to-severe OSA are still at the same high risk for systemic cardiovascular and metabolic complications (stroke, MI, diabetes)", "No, because the sleep study must be wrong", "Yes, but only if they are over 70", "No, they should be observed for 10 years"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Many patients adapt to chronic fatigue and don''t realize how tired they are, or their bodies simply tolerate the sleep fragmentation without feeling sleepy. However, their blood vessels and heart are still suffering the mechanical and hypoxic stress, mandating treatment.')
